# Local env holds a LIVE provider credential — never commit it.
# (On the deployed host there is no such file: the key is a `pass://`
# pointer in the profile, resolved by the daemon. See template/.)
.env
!.env.example

# The project's own virtualenv.  `uv.lock` is COMMITTED, not ignored:
# this is an application that gets deployed, and the lock is the half
# that makes a second host resolve what this one resolved.  Ignoring it
# was the same mistake pyproject.toml exists to fix — a clone resolved
# `jaato-sdk` freely, landed on 0.19.0, and every session was refused.
# `uv sync --frozen` on the VPS failed outright without it.
.venv/
__pycache__/

# A session never runs here, so none of this should ever appear: the repo
# is the implementation and the template, and memories, references,
# documents, recordings and session journals belong in
# `<root>/<caller>/workspace`.  Kept as a safety net against committing
# somebody's second brain if anyone runs `--workspace .`.
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT, and that slash is load-bearing.  Unanchored,
# `.jaato/` matches at EVERY depth, so it also swallowed
# `template/.jaato/` — the authored assets this repo exists to ship.
# Files already tracked kept working, which is what made it invisible:
# only a NEW file was dropped, silently, by `git add -A`.  Measured
# 2026-10-11: `fuentes_reales.py` was written, referenced from
# `_base_escriba.yaml`, committed as part of a change whose other files
# were all tracked, deployed — and on the box the profile named a
# processor that was not there, so EVERY `signal_completion` came back
# "completion_processor could not be located" and no turn could close.
/.jaato/
/docs/
/audio/

# The template is COPIED INTO workspaces, so runtime state must never
# accumulate in it: whatever is here is laid down in the next person's
# workspace.  `workspace.py` skips these on copy as well — belt and
# braces, because one leak is one person reading another's memories.
#
# A KEEP-LIST, NOT A DENY-LIST, which is the direction the framework's
# own generator argues for (`jaato-scaffold explain archetype
# gitignore`): everything under `template/.jaato/` is ignored unless it
# is named here, so a state directory or a `<provider>_auth.json` that a
# later jaato release starts writing is never one `git add -A` from a
# remote.  The cost is that a NEW authored subdirectory needs a line —
# and it fails loudly, as a file that will not commit, which is the one
# failure mode this list is allowed to have.
#
# The un-exclude comes FIRST because git cannot re-include anything
# beneath an excluded directory.
!template/.jaato/
template/.jaato/*
!template/.jaato/agents/
!template/.jaato/completion_schemas/
!template/.jaato/profiles/
!template/.jaato/scripts/
template/docs/
template/audio/
template/.env
